// #90, review round 5: behind a reference nothing converts.
//
// A generic body is not substituted, so `let r: & &T = &p;` is `void ***r =
// &p;` -- and `p = &a` over a parameter at `T = i64` makes `&p` a `long long
// **`. Only the top layer of a reference annotation was asked (where C does
// convert a `void *`), so the layer below it went unasked. Measured on main
// (2ca5eb5) and on round 5 alike: internal compiler error, the C backend
// emitted ill-typed C. Each reference layer is now followed, and the types
// two pointers point to must be one.
fn f<T>(a: T) -> i64 {
    let p = &a;
    let r: & &T = &p;
    7
}

fn main() {
    print_int(f(5));
}
